(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the internal architecture of NumPy's `ndarray` compared to a standard Python list, focusing on memory layout and cache locality.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Architecture Comparison:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Standard Python List \(`list`\):) Tj
T*
(   A Python list is essentially an array of pointers to `PyObject` structures scattered) Tj
T*
(   arbitrarily across the heap. Each integer or float element incurs full object overhead: an) Tj
T*
(   8-byte reference count, an 8-byte type pointer, and the payload value \(consuming ~28 bytes for) Tj
T*
(   an integer\). Accessing list elements requires pointer indirection on every access, causing) Tj
T*
(   severe CPU cache misses and preventing vectorization.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. NumPy ndarray \(`ndarray`\):) Tj
T*
(   An `ndarray` consists of a compact Python wrapper object containing:) Tj
T*
(   - A pointer to a single, contiguous block of raw C memory storing homogeneous primitive data) Tj
T*
(   types \(e.g. 8-byte double-precision IEEE 754 floats\).) Tj
T*
(   - A `shape` tuple defining dimension extents.) Tj
T*
(   - A `strides` tuple defining the byte step size required to advance one element along each) Tj
T*
(   dimension.) Tj
T*
(   - A `dtype` descriptor object specifying data format and endianness.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Cache Locality Benefits:) Tj
T*
(   Because data is laid out contiguously, sequential memory accesses trigger automatic hardware) Tj
T*
(   CPU prefetching, populating L1/L2 cache lines \(64 bytes = 8 double floats per fetch\). This) Tj
T*
(   unlocks modern SIMD \(AVX2/AVX-512\) vector instruction execution, delivering 50x-100x speedups) Tj
T*
(   over Python lists.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the mathematical rules governing NumPy array broadcasting and demonstrate with worked 2D/3D examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Broadcasting Rules:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   When performing element-wise operations on two arrays of shapes $\(d_1, d_2, \\dots, d_n\)$ and) Tj
T*
(   $\(s_1, s_2, \\dots, s_m\)$:) Tj
T*
(   1. Right-Alignment: Prepend dimensions of size 1 to the shape of the array with fewer) Tj
T*
(   dimensions until both shapes have equal rank.) Tj
T*
(   2. Compatibility Condition: Along each dimension, the sizes must either be:) Tj
T*
(   - Exactly equal, or) Tj
T*
(   - One of them is 1.) Tj
T*
(   3. Virtual Expansion: Any dimension of size 1 is virtually stretched \(without copying physical) Tj
T*
(   memory\) to match the larger size by setting its stride along that dimension to 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Worked Example:) Tj
T*
(   Array A: Shape \(4, 1, 6\)) Tj
T*
(   Array B: Shape \(3, 6\)) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Prepend 1 to B -> Shape becomes \(1, 3, 6\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Compare axis by axis from right to left:) Tj
T*
(   - Axis 2: 6 vs 6 -> Equal \(Compatible\)) Tj
T*
(   - Axis 1: 1 vs 3 -> One is 1 \(Compatible, A broadcasts from 1 to 3\)) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
