(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain array memory strides, C-order vs. Fortran-order, and how `strides` enable zero-copy transposition and slicing.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Memory Strides Definition:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Strides are an N-tuple of integers $\(s_0, s_1, \\dots, s_{N-1}\)$ where each $s_i$ indicates the) Tj
T*
(   number of bytes that must be stepped in memory to move to the next adjacent element along axis) Tj
T*
(   $i$.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. C-Contiguous Order \(Row-Major\):) Tj
T*
(   Data is stored row by row. The last index changes fastest in memory.) Tj
T*
(   For shape $\(M, N\)$ and element byte size $B$:) Tj
T*
(   $$\\text{strides} = \(N \\times B, B\)$$) Tj
T*
(   For a $3 \\times 4$ array of `float64` \($B=8$ bytes\):) Tj
T*
(   $$\\text{strides} = \(4 \\times 8, 8\) = \(32, 8\)$$) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Fortran-Contiguous Order \(Column-Major\):) Tj
T*
(   Data is stored column by column. The first index changes fastest:) Tj
T*
(   $$\\text{strides} = \(B, M \\times B\) = \(8, 24\)$$) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Zero-Copy Transposition \(`arr.T`\):) Tj
T*
(   To transpose a 2D array, NumPy simply reverses the shape and strides tuples: $\\text{shape} =) Tj
T*
(   \(N, M\)$ and $\\text{strides} = \(s_1, s_0\)$. No physical array elements are reallocated or copied) Tj
T*
(   in RAM; only the 8-byte stride integers in the Python header are swapped.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the performance and memory implications of slicing views versus advanced indexing copies in NumPy.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Views vs Copies in NumPy:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Basic Slicing \(Views\):) Tj
T*
(   Constructed using slice notation `arr[start:stop:step]` or scalar indices.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanics: Creates a new `ndarray` header pointing to the same existing data buffer with) Tj
T*
(   adjusted offset, shape, and strides.) Tj
T*
(   - Memory: O\(1\) allocation overhead \(~96 bytes for Python header\), zero buffer allocation.) Tj
T*
(   - Mutation: Mutating `view[0] = 99` directly mutates the original array!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Advanced Indexing \(Copies\):) Tj
T*
(   Triggered when indexing with:) Tj
T*
(   - Boolean arrays \(e.g. `arr[arr > 0]`\)) Tj
T*
(   - Integer arrays or lists \(e.g. `arr[[0, 2, 4]]`\)) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanics: Because targeted elements may be scattered arbitrarily in memory, they cannot be) Tj
T*
(   addressed by constant strides. NumPy must allocate an entirely new memory buffer and copy) Tj
T*
(   elements into it.) Tj
T*
(   - Memory & Performance: O\(K\) allocation where K is number of matched elements. Mutating the) Tj
T*
(   copy does NOT affect the original array.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
