(   [Problem Statement]: Implement a sliding-window Short-Time Fourier Transform \(STFT\) in NumPy using `as_strided` and window functions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   STFT divides a long time-varying signal into overlapping segments to compute a spectrogram:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   ```python) Tj
T*
(   import numpy as np) Tj
T*
(   from numpy.lib.stride_tricks import as_strided) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   def compute_stft\(x, nperseg=256, noverlap=128\):) Tj
T*
(   step = nperseg - noverlap) Tj
T*
(   n_frames = \(len\(x\) - nperseg\) // step + 1) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Zero-copy sliding window segmentation using strides) Tj
T*
(   elem_bytes = x.strides[0]) Tj
T*
(   shape = \(n_frames, nperseg\)) Tj
T*
(   strides = \(step * elem_bytes, elem_bytes\)) Tj
T*
(   frames = as_strided\(x, shape=shape, strides=strides\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Apply Hanning window across all frames simultaneously) Tj
T*
(   window = np.hanning\(nperseg\)) Tj
T*
(   windowed_frames = frames * window) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Compute FFT along axis 1) Tj
T*
(   stft_matrix = np.fft.rfft\(windowed_frames, axis=1\)) Tj
T*
(   return stft_matrix) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
